  <section class="section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <p class="label reveal">Frequently asked questions</p>
      <h2 class="section-title reveal">If you searched for this, you're in the right place.</h2>
      <div class="faq reveal" id="faq">
        <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" aria-expanded="false">How do I know if a doctor in Mexico is really board certified?<span class="faq__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="faq__a"><p class="faq__a-inner">In Mexico, board certification is issued by CONACEM (Consejo Nacional de Certificación de Especialidades Médicas). Unlike in the U.S., it expires — typically every five years — and must be renewed. A doctor can have a valid SEP specialty license and still have an expired CONACEM certification, meaning their specialty credentials haven't been reviewed in years. We verify both directly in each registry.</p></div></div>
        <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" aria-expanded="false">What does COFEPRIS do, and what happens if there's a sanction?<span class="faq__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="faq__a"><p class="faq__a-inner">COFEPRIS is Mexico's federal health regulatory authority — roughly the FDA's enforcement role. They issue alerts, sanctions, and can order clinic closures. We report what type of alert exists, when it was issued, and what it referred to — so you can judge the severity yourself.</p></div></div>
        <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" aria-expanded="false">What's the difference between a cédula profesional and a cédula de especialidad?<span class="faq__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="faq__a"><p class="faq__a-inner">A cédula profesional confirms a completed medical degree and authorization to practice. A cédula de especialidad confirms a recognized specialty residency. A doctor can have the first without the second — a licensed physician, but not a credentialed specialist in your procedure. We verify both independently.</p></div></div>
        <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" aria-expanded="false">What happens if I have a complication during surgery in Mexico?<span class="faq__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="faq__a"><p class="faq__a-inner">It depends almost entirely on how far your clinic is from a hospital with an ICU — sometimes 5 minutes, sometimes 40. Your report includes the specific nearest hospital, the estimated transfer time, and the nearest U.S. or Canadian consulate, which can provide a list of local physicians, help with emergency communication, and assist with repatriation.</p></div></div>
        <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" aria-expanded="false">Is it safe to get surgery in Mexico?<span class="faq__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="faq__a"><p class="faq__a-inner">That depends entirely on the specific doctor and clinic — which is exactly why verification exists. Mexico has JCI-accredited hospitals and surgeons trained at leading U.S. institutions. It also has unlicensed practitioners and clinics without current permits. The country isn't the variable. The specific provider is.</p></div></div>
        <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" aria-expanded="false">How do I verify a Mexican doctor if I don't speak Spanish?<span class="faq__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="faq__a"><p class="faq__a-inner">All the registries are in Spanish — CONACEM, the SEP registry, and the COFEPRIS alert system have no English version. That's the core reason this service exists. We navigate those systems, interpret what we find, and deliver the report in plain English.</p></div></div>
        <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" aria-expanded="false">Can a doctor buy or fake credentials in Mexico?<span class="faq__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="faq__a"><p class="faq__a-inner">SEP licenses carry a unique registration number verifiable in the federal registry, so the credential itself can't be faked — but someone can present a real credential belonging to a similarly named person, or present a legitimate one while omitting that their specialty certification has expired. Our homonym check and cross-verification are designed to catch exactly that.</p></div></div>
        <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" aria-expanded="false">How long does it take to receive the report?<span class="faq__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="faq__a"><p class="faq__a-inner">24-48 hours from confirmed payment. Submit Friday evening, expect it by Sunday. Premium includes priority 24h delivery if you need it faster.</p></div></div>
        <div class="faq__item"><button class="faq__q" aria-expanded="false">What if you find something serious?<span class="faq__icon" aria-hidden="true">+</span></button><div class="faq__a"><p class="faq__a-inner">We report what we find. If it's significant — an active sanction, an expired certification, a closure, a pattern of serious complaints — the report explains clearly what it means. We don't make the decision for you, but you'll have the information to make it yourself.</p></div></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

